<h3>Page Count and Service Complexity</h3>

<p>Every page on your website costs money to design, write, and build. A ten-page site and a thirty-page site can differ by $3,000 to $5,000 at the agency level. Here is what typical contractor sites include:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Core pages (5–7):</strong> Home, About, Services, Service Areas, Reviews, Contact, Blog</li>
  <li><strong>Service pages (3–10):</strong> One page per major service — roof repair, roof replacement, gutter installation, siding, etc.</li>
  <li><strong>Service area pages (5–30):</strong> One page per city or neighborhood you serve — critical for <a href="/services/local-seo">local SEO</a></li>
  <li><strong>Supporting pages (2–5):</strong> Financing, warranties, FAQs, careers, privacy policy</li>
</ul>

<p>A roofing contractor serving five cities might need 15 to 20 pages. An HVAC contractor serving 15 cities with heating, cooling, and indoor air quality services might need 30 to 40 pages. Each additional service area page adds $200 to $500 in design, copy, and SEO setup costs. But skipping these pages is expensive in a different way. Contractors with dedicated service area pages rank higher in local search. Data from the home services industry shows that 73 percent of contractors with service area pages rank higher than those without them.</p>

<h3>Design Customization vs. Templates</h3>

<p>Custom design costs $2,000 to $5,000 more than template customization. The difference is not just aesthetics. A custom design system is built around your specific conversion goals. It places click-to-call buttons where mobile users actually tap. It structures trust signals — licenses, insurance, reviews, before-and-after photos — in the order that research shows builds confidence. It optimizes form placement to reduce friction.</p>

<p>Templates, by contrast, are designed to be generic. They look fine, but they are not optimized for the specific psychology of a homeowner who just discovered a leak at 10 PM and needs to decide whether to call you or your competitor. That decision happens in under three seconds. Custom design wins those three seconds.</p>

<h3>Copywriting and Photography</h3>

<p>Professional copywriting for a contractor website costs $1,000 to $3,000. Cheap copy is obvious. It reads like every other contractor website: "We are a family-owned business with over 20 years of experience serving the greater metro area with quality workmanship and customer satisfaction." That sentence says nothing. It does not address the homeowner's fear. It does not differentiate you from the competition. It does not include the local keywords that help you rank.</p>

<p>Professional copywriters who specialize in contractors write for two audiences simultaneously: the human reader and the search engine. They weave in location-specific terms naturally. They structure headlines around the questions homeowners actually search: "How much does roof repair cost in Dallas?" not "Our Roofing Services." They write calls-to-action that create urgency: "Get a Free Inspection Before the Next Storm" not "Contact Us."</p>

<p>Photography costs $500 to $2,000. Real job photos outperform stock photography by a wide margin. Research shows that 38 percent of homeowners are influenced by before-and-after photos when choosing a contractor. Stock photos of generic houses and smiling families signal inauthenticity. Real photos of your actual crews, your actual trucks, and your actual completed jobs build trust. A professional photographer for a half-day shoot runs $800 to $1,500 and provides images you will use across your website, <a href="/services/gbp-optimization">Google Business Profile</a>, social media, and proposals for years.</p>

<h3>Technical SEO and Performance</h3>

<p>Technical SEO setup at launch costs $500 to $2,000. This includes title tags, meta descriptions, header structure, image alt text, schema markup for local business and services, XML sitemaps, and Google Search Console setup. Without this foundation, your site is invisible to Google. With it, you have a fighting chance to rank for "roofing contractor near me" and related terms.</p>

<p>Performance optimization — compressing images, minifying code, configuring caching, setting up a content delivery network — is often bundled into agency builds but costs $500 to $1,500 as a standalone service. A site that loads in under two seconds on mobile converts at roughly double the rate of a site that loads in four seconds. That difference alone can justify the investment.</p>

