<h2>Contractor Website Pricing by Website Type and Business Size</h2>

<p>The contractor website cost that makes sense for you depends entirely on where your business sits right now. A solo plumber with one truck has different needs, different budgets, and different ROI math than a roofing company with 15 crews and a $5 million annual revenue target. This section breaks down pricing by business stage so you can match your investment to your situation.</p>

<h3>Solo Operators and Startups (1 Truck, Under $250K Revenue)</h3>

<p>If you are just starting out or running a one-person operation, your website needs to do three things: prove you are legitimate, make it easy to contact you, and show up when someone searches for your trade in your city. You do not need 30 pages. You need a clean, fast, mobile-optimized site with your phone number, your service areas, your license information, and a few photos of your work.</p>

<p>At this stage, the most cost-effective option is a <strong>managed website subscription</strong> at $200 to $400 per month with no upfront cost. Providers like YourSites and Molu Agency offer contractor-specific templates, hosting, maintenance, and basic SEO for a flat monthly fee. You get a live site within days, not weeks. The trade-off is that you do not own the site — if you cancel, it disappears. For a startup testing the market, that risk is acceptable.</p>

<p>Alternatively, a <strong>template agency build</strong> at $3,000 to $5,000 gives you ownership and a more customized look. You pay upfront, but you own the asset. Over three years, the total cost is roughly the same as a subscription model. The difference is cash flow: subscription spreads the cost, while an upfront build requires capital.</p>

<p>DIY builders are tempting at $15 to $50 per month, but the hidden costs are significant. The time you spend building and maintaining the site is time you are not billing. At $75 per hour, 30 hours of DIY work costs $2,250 in lost revenue. Plus, DIY sites convert poorly. A solo operator relying on a Wix site for <a href="/services/lead-generation">lead generation</a> is likely leaving money on the table.</p>

<h3>Growing Contractors (2–5 Trucks, $250K–$1M Revenue)</h3>

<p>At this stage, your website is no longer a digital business card. It is a lead generation machine. You are probably running Google Ads, investing in SEO, or both. Your website needs to convert paid traffic efficiently and rank organically for service-related searches. A slow, generic site wastes your ad budget and limits your organic growth.</p>

<p>The right investment here is a <strong>semi-custom agency build</strong> at $5,000 to $10,000. This gets you:</p>

<ul>
  <li>A design system built for contractors, not a generic template</li>
  <li>10 to 20 pages including service pages and service area pages</li>
  <li>Professional copywriting optimized for local SEO</li>
  <li>Click-to-call buttons, quote forms, and live chat integration</li>
  <li>Google Business Profile synchronization</li>
  <li>Review widget integration showing live Google reviews</li>
  <li>90+ mobile PageSpeed score</li>
  <li>Schema markup for local business, services, and reviews</li>
  <li>Analytics and conversion tracking setup</li>
</ul>

<p>Ongoing costs at this stage run $300 to $600 per month for hosting, maintenance, security updates, and minor content changes. If you add an SEO retainer, budget another $1,000 to $2,500 per month. Total year-one investment: $10,000 to $25,000. That sounds like a lot until you run the ROI math. If your average job is $3,000 and your website generates just two extra jobs per month, it pays for itself in under six months.</p>

<h3>Established Contractors (5–15 Trucks, $1M–$5M Revenue)</h3>

<p>Established contractors competing in metro markets face intense online competition. Your website needs to outrank and out-convert every other contractor in your service area. That requires depth: 30 to 50 pages, extensive service area coverage, advanced conversion features, and ongoing optimization.</p>

<p>At this level, budget <strong>$8,000 to $18,000</strong> for initial development and <strong>$500 to $1,500 per month</strong> for ongoing support and SEO. The site should include:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Custom design with brand-specific visual identity</li>
  <li>20 to 40 service and service area pages</li>
  <li>Professional photography and videography</li>
  <li>Advanced lead capture: multi-step forms, financing calculators, appointment booking</li>
  <li>CRM integration (ServiceTitan, Housecall Pro, JobNimbus)</li>
  <li>Call tracking and attribution (CallRail, WhatConverts)</li>
  <li>A/B testing infrastructure</li>
  <li>Content hub with blog and resource library</li>
  <li>Multi-location support if applicable</li>
</ul>

<p>The ROI at this stage is compounding. An established contractor with strong organic rankings and optimized conversion can generate 50 to 100 qualified leads per month from the website alone. At a 20 percent close rate and a $4,000 average ticket, that is $40,000 to $80,000 in monthly revenue from a $1,500 per month investment.</p>

<h3>Multi-Location and Franchise Operations (15+ Trucks, $5M+ Revenue)</h3>

<p>Multi-location contractors need enterprise-grade websites with location finders, individual location pages, franchisee sub-sites, and centralized lead routing. These builds start at $15,000 and routinely exceed $30,000. Ongoing costs include dedicated development support, content management across locations, and advanced analytics dashboards.</p>

<p>The complexity here is not just technical. It is organizational. Each location needs its own local SEO strategy, its own review management, and its own service area content. A franchise with 10 locations might need 200 to 300 pages total. The investment is substantial, but so is the return. A well-executed multi-location website strategy can generate millions in annual revenue while reducing per-lead costs by 40 to 60 percent compared to marketplace leads.</p>

