<h2>The Hidden Costs Nobody Tells You About</h2>

<p>The sticker price on a contractor website proposal is rarely the full story. Hidden costs can add $2,000 to $10,000 to your total investment over the first three years. Some of these are legitimate expenses. Others are traps set by providers who know you will not ask the right questions upfront. This section exposes every hidden cost so you can budget accurately and avoid nasty surprises.</p>

<h3>Domain Name Ownership Traps</h3>

<p>Your domain name — yourcompanyname.com — costs $12 to $20 per year. That is not the hidden cost. The hidden cost is <strong>who owns it</strong>. Some agencies register your domain under their own account, not yours. They bill you annually, but if you ever want to leave, they control the domain. The one on your trucks, your business cards, and your Google Business Profile. Getting it back can take months or require legal action.</p>

<p>Before you sign anything, verify that you are listed as the registrant on the domain. Use a WHOIS lookup tool to check. If the agency is the registrant, insist on a transfer or register the domain yourself before the project starts.</p>

<h3>Hosting Markups and Performance Limits</h3>

<p>Hosting for a contractor website costs $15 to $50 per month for quality shared hosting, or $50 to $200 per month for managed WordPress hosting. Some agencies charge $200 to $500 per month for hosting that costs them $20. They bundle in "maintenance" and "support" to justify the markup, but the actual hosting value is minimal.</p>

<p>Worse, cheap hosting destroys performance. A $5 per month shared hosting plan puts your site on a server with hundreds of other websites. When one of those sites gets a traffic spike, your site slows down. Google measures site speed as a ranking factor. A slow site ranks lower, converts worse, and wastes your ad budget.</p>

<p>Ask your provider exactly what hosting plan they use, what server specifications it includes, and what the actual hosting cost is versus what they charge you. Quality managed WordPress hosting from WP Engine, Kinsta, or Flywheel costs $30 to $100 per month and delivers the speed and reliability your business needs.</p>

<h3>Plugin License Renewals</h3>

<p>WordPress websites rely on plugins for functionality: contact forms, SEO tools, security scanning, backups, caching, image optimization, and more. Premium plugins cost $50 to $300 per year each. A typical contractor website uses five to ten premium plugins. That is $250 to $1,500 per year in plugin licenses that many providers never mention in the initial quote.</p>

<p>When an agency says "we include all necessary plugins," ask specifically which plugins and whether the licenses are lifetime or annual. If they are annual, confirm who pays for renewals in year two and beyond. Some agencies let licenses lapse, leaving your site vulnerable to security issues or broken functionality.</p>

<h3>Content Update Fees</h3>

<p>Some agencies charge $50 to $150 per content update. Change your phone number? $75. Add a new service? $150. Update your team photo? $100. At those rates, you will avoid making updates. Your site goes stale. Your rankings drop. Your competitors pass you.</p>

<p>A better model is unlimited content updates included in a monthly maintenance plan at $200 to $400 per month. You can make changes whenever you need without worrying about the bill. This encourages you to keep your site current, which helps SEO and conversion.</p>

<h3>SSL Certificates and Security</h3>

<p>SSL certificates — the padlock icon in the browser bar — are free through Let's Encrypt on any quality host. Some providers charge $50 to $150 per year for SSL as a separate line item. That is pure profit for them. If your provider charges for SSL, question what else they are marking up.</p>

<p>Security monitoring and malware removal are legitimate ongoing costs. Quality security services like Sucuri or Wordfence Premium cost $100 to $300 per year. These are worth paying for. A hacked website that serves malware to visitors will get blacklisted by Google and destroy your search rankings overnight.</p>

<h3>Early Termination Penalties</h3>

<p>Long-term contracts with cancellation fees are a red flag. A 12-month or 24-month lock-in with a $500 to $2,000 early termination fee means the provider is not confident you will stay voluntarily. Confident providers offer month-to-month agreements because they know their work keeps clients.</p>

<p>Before signing, ask: "What happens if I want to cancel after three months?" If the answer involves penalties, negotiation, or guilt trips, walk away. The best agencies earn your business every month.</p>

<h3>Stock Photography vs. Real Photography</h3>

<p>Many proposals include "professional photography" that turns out to be stock images from Shutterstock or Unsplash. Stock photos cost $10 to $50 each. Real photography costs $800 to $1,500 for a half-day shoot. If your proposal mentions photography, ask explicitly whether it includes a photographer visiting your job sites or just stock image licensing.</p>

<h3>Migration Costs</h3>

<p>If you outgrow a DIY builder or a bad agency and need to migrate to a new platform, expect to pay $2,000 to $4,000 for the migration alone. Content needs to be extracted, reformatted, and repopulated. URLs need redirects to preserve SEO value. Forms need rebuilding. This is a cost you pay once, but it is entirely avoidable if you choose the right platform from the start.</p>

