<h3>Total Cost of Ownership: A Three-Year View</h3>

<p>Smart contractors think in three-year increments, not one-year budgets. Here is what a quality semi-custom contractor website actually costs over three years:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Year 1:</strong> $8,000 build + $4,800 maintenance + $600 plugin renewals + $200 domain + $1,000 photography = $14,600</li>
  <li><strong>Year 2:</strong> $4,800 maintenance + $600 plugin renewals + $200 domain = $5,600</li>
  <li><strong>Year 3:</strong> $4,800 maintenance + $600 plugin renewals + $200 domain = $5,600</li>
  <li><strong>Three-year total:</strong> $25,800</li>
</ul>

<p>That is $717 per month over three years for a website that generates leads 24/7. Compare that to Angi or HomeAdvisor, where shared leads cost $45 to $180 each and you are competing with three to five other contractors for every lead. Your website delivers exclusive leads at a fraction of the cost.</p>

<h2>The ROI of a Contractor Website: Why Cheap Sites Cost More</h2>

<p>The biggest mistake contractors make when evaluating website cost is looking at the price tag instead of the return. A $3,000 website that generates zero leads is infinitely more expensive than a $10,000 website that generates $100,000 in annual revenue. This section breaks down the actual ROI of contractor websites with real numbers, real scenarios, and a framework you can use to evaluate any proposal.</p>

<h3>How Contractor Website ROI Works</h3>

<p>Website ROI is simple math. You need four numbers:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Monthly website visitors:</strong> How many people find your site through search, ads, and direct traffic</li>
  <li><strong>Conversion rate:</strong> What percentage of visitors contact you</li>
  <li><strong>Lead-to-customer close rate:</strong> What percentage of contacts become paying customers</li>
  <li><strong>Average job value:</strong> How much revenue each customer generates</li>
</ol>

<p>Multiply these together and you get monthly revenue from your website. Subtract your website costs and you get net ROI.</p>

<p>Here is a real example. A roofing contractor in Dallas gets 500 website visitors per month. Their DIY Wix site converts at 0.5 percent, generating 2.5 leads per month. They close 30 percent of leads at an average job value of $8,000. Monthly revenue: $6,000. Annual revenue: $72,000.</p>

<p>Now they invest $8,000 in a semi-custom agency build with <a href="/services/conversion-optimization">conversion optimization</a>. The new site loads in 1.8 seconds, has click-to-call buttons above the fold, displays live Google reviews, and includes trust signals throughout. Conversion jumps to 3 percent. Same 500 visitors now generate 15 leads per month. Same 30 percent close rate. Monthly revenue: $36,000. Annual revenue: $432,000.</p>

<p>The website upgrade generated an additional $360,000 in annual revenue. The $8,000 investment paid for itself in the first week. That is not a typo. One week.</p>

