<h3>Organic Leads vs. Marketplace Leads: The Cost Comparison</h3>

<p>Here is where website ROI gets really interesting. Compare the cost of leads from your own website versus leads from marketplaces like Angi, HomeAdvisor, or Thumbtack.</p>

<p>Data from the home services industry shows that organic website leads cost an average of $22 per lead. Google Local Services Ads leads cost $85 per lead. Social media ads cost $45 per lead. HomeAdvisor and Angi shared leads cost $120 per lead. But the real difference is not the cost per lead. It is the close rate.</p>

<p>Website leads are exclusive. The homeowner found you, researched you, and chose to contact you. Close rates for exclusive website leads range from 25 to 35 percent. Marketplace leads are shared with three to five competitors. The homeowner is getting multiple calls. Close rates for shared leads range from 10 to 15 percent.</p>

<p>Let us calculate true cost per booked job:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Organic website lead:</strong> $22 cost per lead × 25% close rate = $88 per booked job</li>
  <li><strong>Google LSA lead:</strong> $85 cost per lead × 18% close rate = $472 per booked job</li>
  <li><strong>Social media lead:</strong> $45 cost per lead × 8% close rate = $563 per booked job</li>
  <li><strong>HomeAdvisor/Angi lead:</strong> $120 cost per lead × 12% close rate = $1,000 per booked job</li>
</ul>

<p>Your website generates booked jobs at one-tenth the cost of marketplace leads. That is not a minor efficiency. That is a competitive advantage that compounds every month.</p>

<h3>Speed to Lead: The Hidden Revenue Killer</h3>

<p>Response time is the single biggest factor in lead conversion that most contractors ignore. Research from InsideSales.com shows that responding to a lead within five minutes makes you 21 times more likely to qualify that lead than if you wait 30 minutes. The average contractor responds in 24 hours. That means the average contractor is losing 95 percent of their potential conversions to competitors who respond faster.</p>

<p>A well-built contractor website includes automated lead response: instant confirmation emails, SMS notifications to your phone, CRM integration that triggers follow-up sequences, and after-hours capture that books appointments while you sleep. These features add $500 to $1,500 to the build cost but recover $8,000 to $15,000 per month in otherwise lost revenue.</p>

<h3>The Compounding Effect of SEO</h3>

<p>Website ROI compounds over time. A new site might generate 10 leads per month in month one. By month 12, with consistent SEO and content, that same site might generate 50 leads per month. The upfront investment stays fixed while the return grows. This is why contractors who invest in quality websites early see exponential returns in years two and three.</p>

<p>Data from BrightLocal shows that contractors who optimize their Google Business Profile and website together see a 156 percent average increase in traffic. Contractors outside the top three local search results get 5 to 10 percent of the clicks. Contractors in the top three get 60 to 70 percent. The difference between position four and position one is not marginal. It is the difference between a struggling business and a thriving one.</p>

