<h2>How to Choose the Right Contractor Website Provider</h2>

<p>Choosing a website provider is a high-stakes decision. The wrong choice costs you thousands in hidden fees, lost leads, and eventually a complete rebuild. The right choice becomes a revenue-generating asset that pays for itself within months. This section gives you a decision framework with specific questions to ask, red flags to watch for, and a scoring system to compare providers objectively.</p>

<h3>The Seven Questions That Separate Professionals from Pretenders</h3>

<p>Every contractor website provider should be able to answer these seven questions clearly and specifically. Vague answers, deflection, or pressure tactics are immediate red flags.</p>

<p><strong>Question 1: Can I see contractor websites you have built in my trade?</strong></p>

<p>A provider who builds websites for dentists, lawyers, and restaurants is not a contractor specialist. They do not understand the homeowner psychology, the emergency service urgency, or the local SEO dynamics that drive contractor leads. Ask for three to five live examples in your specific trade. Visit those sites on your phone. Click the call button. Fill out the form. See how long it takes to get a response. If the provider cannot show you relevant examples, they are learning on your dime.</p>

<p><strong>Question 2: What is your process from kickoff to launch?</strong></p>

<p>A professional agency has a documented process: discovery, strategy, design, development, content, review, launch, and post-launch optimization. Ask for a timeline with specific milestones. A vague answer like "usually takes a few weeks" means they wing it. A detailed answer like "week 1 is discovery and keyword research, week 2 is wireframes, weeks 3 to 4 are design, weeks 5 to 8 are development, week 9 is content and review, week 10 is launch" means they have done this before.</p>

<p><strong>Question 3: Who writes the content?</strong></p>

<p>Content is half the battle. A beautiful site with bad copy converts poorly. Ask whether the provider writes the content, whether it is original or templated, and whether it is optimized for local SEO. If they say "you provide the content," that is a $1,000 to $3,000 hidden cost you need to add to your budget. If they say "we use AI to generate content," run. AI-generated content is detectable by search engines, often inaccurate, and reads like every other generic contractor site.</p>

<p><strong>Question 4: What happens after launch?</strong></p>

<p>Websites are not set-it-and-forget-it assets. They need updates, security patches, performance monitoring, and content refreshes. Ask what ongoing support includes, what it costs, and what happens if something breaks. A provider with no ongoing support plan is building you a ticking time bomb. WordPress sites without regular updates get hacked. Plugins without updates break. Content without refreshes goes stale and drops in rankings.</p>

<p><strong>Question 5: Do I own everything?</strong></p>

<p>This is the most important question. Ask specifically: Do I own the domain? Do I own the website files? Can I take the site to another provider if I want? Can I edit the content myself? If any answer is no, you are renting, not buying. Renting is fine if you understand the trade-off, but most contractors do not realize they are renting until they try to leave.</p>

<p><strong>Question 6: What is included in the price?</strong></p>

<p>Get a detailed line-item proposal. Design, development, copywriting, photography, SEO setup, plugin licenses, hosting, maintenance, training, support — each should be listed with its cost. A single lump sum with no breakdown is a license to add hidden fees later.</p>

<p><strong>Question 7: What guarantees do you offer?</strong></p>

<p>No agency can guarantee rankings — Google controls the algorithm. But a professional agency can guarantee specific deliverables: a 90+ mobile PageSpeed score, a launch timeline, a certain number of pages, specific functionality, and a revision process. Ask what happens if they miss deadlines or deliverables. A provider with no accountability mechanism is not confident in their own work.</p>

<h3>Red Flags: When to Walk Away</h3>

<p>These warning signs indicate a provider you should avoid, regardless of how low their price is:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Guaranteed #1 rankings:</strong> Impossible promise. Usually involves black-hat SEO that gets your site penalized.</li>
  <li><strong>No contract or vague contract:</strong> You have no recourse if they disappear with your deposit.</li>
  <li><strong>Pressure to sign today:</strong> Legitimate providers do not use high-pressure sales tactics.</li>
  <li><strong>No portfolio or fake portfolio:</strong> Stock images labeled as "client work" are common in low-end markets.</li>
  <li><strong>Payment upfront with no milestones:</strong> Never pay 100 percent before work begins. A 50/50 or 33/33/33 payment structure is standard.</li>
  <li><strong>No local SEO strategy:</strong> A contractor website without local SEO is a billboard in the desert.</li>
  <li><strong>Generic templates presented as custom:</strong> Ask to see the template before they customize it. If it looks like 500 other sites, it is a template.</li>
</ul>

